WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:11.175
This is Chapter 10, the last one: drift
and evidence for the security officer.

4
00:00:11.175 --> 00:00:14.938
A register approved once proves
only the day it was approved.

5
00:00:14.938 --> 00:00:18.046
The security officer needs
to know what changed since,

6
00:00:18.046 --> 00:00:21.500
who changed it, and that nobody
edited the record afterwards.

7
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.847
We'll start from the Sentinel rule
Learn links for added credentials,

8
00:00:25.847 --> 00:00:30.728
see what it misses, then walk the drift
run, the approved baseline, the rules, the

9
00:00:30.728 --> 00:00:35.762
review cycle, the vintage comparison and
the hash chain, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:41.270
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.870
Why does this chapter come last?

12
00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.662
Because everything before
it produces state,

13
00:00:49.662 --> 00:00:52.308
and state proves only
the moment it was read.

14
00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:55.358
Four sources already say
what the evidence is for.

15
00:00:55.358 --> 00:00:58.051
The plan asks for scheduled drift checks,

16
00:00:58.051 --> 00:01:01.695
a full review cycle, and
evidence for specific controls.

17
00:01:01.695 --> 00:01:04.447
Book 2 sets the pattern:
commit a baseline,

18
00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:08.333
compare every run with it, and
close findings at the source.

19
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.546
The companion guide maps the
controls, while the assessor decides.

20
00:01:12.546 --> 00:01:15.308
And Book 17 adds the AI side.

21
00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.395
The code's job is to turn state,

22
00:01:17.395 --> 00:01:22.010
events and an approved baseline
into evidence with no gaps.

23
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.273
Learn gives the facts the design turns on.

24
00:01:28.273 --> 00:01:31.282
A credential added to an
existing app is high risk,

25
00:01:31.282 --> 00:01:35.247
and one added outside the expected
process could be an attacker's.

26
00:01:35.247 --> 00:01:40.607
Learn names the exact audit events, keeps
the log for thirty days with a premium

27
00:01:40.607 --> 00:01:45.884
license, and defines expiring as within
thirty days and unused as over ninety.

28
00:01:45.884 --> 00:01:49.410
For this estate, Graph is
the worldwide endpoint.

29
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:54.788
One report, the service principal sign-in
activity, is beta and worldwide only,

30
00:01:54.788 --> 00:01:58.485
so in the higher clouds the
code records it as not read.

31
00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:01.990
And the other chapters feed
in through a small contract.

32
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.215
Microsoft's starting point is the security
operations guide for applications.

33
00:02:10.215 --> 00:02:13.727
It says what to watch, where
to find it in the audit log,

34
00:02:13.727 --> 00:02:17.240
and when to alert: outside
normal hours or workflows.

35
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.922
And it links a ready-made
Sentinel rule for new

36
00:02:19.922 --> 00:02:22.815
credentials added to an
app or service principal.

37
00:02:22.815 --> 00:02:26.191
That rule lives in Microsoft's
Sentinel repository,

38
00:02:26.191 --> 00:02:30.352
so it's pinned unmodified, with
checked hashes and a weekly watch.

39
00:02:30.352 --> 00:02:33.713
A harness then splits
the query into its twenty

40
00:02:33.713 --> 00:02:37.590
stages and runs each one over
ten fictional audit rows.

41
00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.383
An unfamiliar stage throws,

42
00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:43.890
so an upstream change fails the
tests rather than being half-run.

43
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.241
Run over the fixture, the rule
does catch the obvious case:

44
00:02:51.241 --> 00:02:55.302
a person adding a secret to an app
that already had a certificate.

45
00:02:55.302 --> 00:02:58.853
But three filters make it
blind in the riskiest places.

46
00:02:58.853 --> 00:03:03.152
It only looks at initiators with
an at sign, so a credential added

47
00:03:03.152 --> 00:03:07.614
by an app is never seen, including
an app abusing a broad permission.

48
00:03:07.614 --> 00:03:10.863
It drops events where the
old key list was empty,

49
00:03:10.863 --> 00:03:13.777
so the very first secret
on an app vanishes.

50
00:03:13.777 --> 00:03:16.154
And it keeps only application targets,

51
00:03:16.154 --> 00:03:20.150
so a credential added to a
service principal is dropped too.

52
00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.300
The other gaps are about
noise, privacy and scope.

53
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.882
The rule knows nothing about the pipeline,

54
00:03:29.882 --> 00:03:34.162
so every legitimate rotation alerts,
and soon nobody reads the alerts.

55
00:03:34.162 --> 00:03:37.137
It parses only the first key in an event.

56
00:03:37.137 --> 00:03:40.787
It carries people's names into
the alert, wherever that goes.

57
00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:44.660
And it needs a Sentinel workspace
before any evidence exists,

58
00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.001
while covering only one of
the plan's five drift items.

59
00:03:48.001 --> 00:03:51.625
So the verdict: use the rule's
activity names and its key

60
00:03:51.625 --> 00:03:55.780
comparison as the model, and
don't use it as the drift check.

61
00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:00.955
So what does the code build?

62
00:04:00.955 --> 00:04:02.979
One run, written as one folder:

63
00:04:02.979 --> 00:04:07.450
Chapter 1's inventory and Chapter
2's ownership check, unchanged,

64
00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:13.017
plus this chapter's drift folder and a run
record that ties them together by hash.

65
00:04:13.017 --> 00:04:17.592
A task that fails is recorded as an
error, never as an empty result.

66
00:04:17.592 --> 00:04:22.019
The baseline is a fingerprint per
identity over twenty governance columns,

67
00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:25.109
leaving out things like last
logon that change daily,

68
00:04:25.109 --> 00:04:28.130
and it counts only once a
reviewer approves its hash.

69
00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:31.596
The audit read is narrow:
declared calls only,

70
00:04:31.596 --> 00:04:35.520
thirty days at most, and
identifiers, not names.

71
00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.370
Nine drift rules run against
the approved baseline.

72
00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:47.220
New and changed identities are warnings,
with the exact columns that changed.

73
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.398
Ownerless identities,
passwords that never expire,

74
00:04:50.398 --> 00:04:53.945
and credentials added outside
the pipeline are findings.

75
00:04:53.945 --> 00:04:57.932
Unused and expiring use
Learn's ninety and thirty days.

76
00:04:57.932 --> 00:05:00.044
A new broad grant is a finding.

77
00:05:00.044 --> 00:05:02.484
And the ninth rule is the honest one:

78
00:05:02.484 --> 00:05:06.520
any gap in the evidence is
reported, never treated as a pass.

79
00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.729
The key rule is what counts as the
pipeline: both a listed identity

80
00:05:10.729 --> 00:05:15.140
and a logged key, so a stolen
pipeline credential still shows up.

81
00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.990
Two more kinds of evidence
sit beside the drift.

82
00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.725
The review cycle belongs to the identity
governance officer: the reviews

83
00:05:26.725 --> 00:05:31.228
are built in Chapters 7 and 9, and
this chapter only reads their records.

84
00:05:31.228 --> 00:05:34.951
It flags an app whose owners
haven't reviewed it this quarter,

85
00:05:34.951 --> 00:05:39.940
an agent whose sponsor hasn't attested this
year, and agent access with no end date.

86
00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:43.465
And crucially, a missing
record isn't a passed review.

87
00:05:43.465 --> 00:05:47.798
Then once a year, the AI use-case
inventory is compared with last year's,

88
00:05:47.798 --> 00:05:50.230
catching new systems with no identity,

89
00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:54.160
and retired ones whose identities
are still switched on.

90
00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.673
Here's what the security
officer actually receives.

91
00:06:00.673 --> 00:06:05.399
Each run is packaged with a summary
that carries rule IDs and fingerprints

92
00:06:05.399 --> 00:06:09.822
but no names, and a checksum file
anyone can verify with a standard tool.

93
00:06:09.822 --> 00:06:13.348
Before packaging, the run
itself is checked end to end.

94
00:06:13.348 --> 00:06:18.307
Each package records the previous package's
hash, so the packages form a chain,

95
00:06:18.307 --> 00:06:21.948
and a replaced package or a
missing day shows up as a break.

96
00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:25.000
Then a period summary
verifies every package,

97
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.097
checks for gaps, and maps findings
to each control as evidence.

98
00:06:29.097 --> 00:06:33.080
The assessor, not the
code, decides each control.

99
00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:37.880
Who's involved?

100
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:41.680
Developers and pipeline engineers
own the scheduled checks.

101
00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.867
The identity governance officer owns
the review cycle and the evidence,

102
00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:47.542
and signs the period summary.

103
00:06:47.542 --> 00:06:49.700
And the security officer receives it,

104
00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.667
verifies it with nothing more than
a checksum tool, and signs the gate.

105
00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:55.855
The permissions are deliberately small.

106
00:06:55.855 --> 00:06:59.234
This chapter adds only the
audit log read; the whole run,

107
00:06:59.234 --> 00:07:03.288
together with Chapters 1 and 2,
needs four Graph read permissions,

108
00:07:03.288 --> 00:07:07.343
Reader on the managed identities,
and the default directory read.

109
00:07:07.343 --> 00:07:08.830
None of it can write.

110
00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.730
Lab 1 needs PowerShell and Python
on a laptop, and nothing else.

111
00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.732
First, run Microsoft's
rule over the fixture:

112
00:07:19.732 --> 00:07:23.568
of four credentials added on
the second day, it finds one.

113
00:07:23.568 --> 00:07:27.817
Then confirm the run needs four read
permissions and nothing that writes.

114
00:07:27.817 --> 00:07:30.061
Run the first day with no baseline,

115
00:07:30.061 --> 00:07:33.267
and it writes a baseline
candidate with its hash.

116
00:07:33.267 --> 00:07:37.968
In real life a second person reviews
that candidate and passes you the hash.

117
00:07:37.968 --> 00:07:41.377
Then run the second day
against the approved baseline,

118
00:07:41.377 --> 00:07:45.900
and the drift appears: more findings
and more warnings than day one.

119
00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:54.809
Reading day two's drift, the three credentials
the rule couldn't see are all there:

120
00:07:54.809 --> 00:07:59.498
one added by a person, one added by
another app abusing a broad permission,

121
00:07:59.498 --> 00:08:01.687
and the very first secret on an app.

122
00:08:01.687 --> 00:08:06.080
Just as important is what's missing:
the pipeline's own two rotations,

123
00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:09.138
because both the identity
and the key log agree.

124
00:08:09.138 --> 00:08:13.351
An expiring certificate from day
one is gone, because it was rotated.

125
00:08:13.351 --> 00:08:16.675
A chapter file with a forged
fingerprint is refused.

126
00:08:16.675 --> 00:08:21.265
Then the vintage comparison finds a
new deployed agent with no identity,

127
00:08:21.265 --> 00:08:24.680
and a retired use case
whose app is still enabled.

128
00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:30.255
Now switch to the Python side.

129
00:08:30.255 --> 00:08:33.611
Package both days, the
second chained to the first:

130
00:08:33.611 --> 00:08:38.767
nineteen findings opened on day two,
and one closed, the rotated certificate.

131
00:08:38.767 --> 00:08:42.488
Verify the package with the code
and with a plain checksum tool,

132
00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:44.992
and confirm no names
appear in the summary.

133
00:08:44.992 --> 00:08:47.768
Then assemble the period
for the security officer.

134
00:08:47.768 --> 00:08:53.143
It's honestly incomplete: some controls
wait on other chapters, and Gate 7

135
00:08:53.143 --> 00:08:58.429
isn't met yet, with passwords that
never expire, orphans, and open reviews.

136
00:08:58.429 --> 00:09:01.380
Finally, break it on purpose: edit a file,

137
00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.420
or swap the order of days,
and verification fails.

138
00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:14.008
Lab 2 uses a test tenant with fictional
people, and the run only reads.

139
00:09:14.008 --> 00:09:17.277
Register a reader with
exactly the four permissions,

140
00:09:17.277 --> 00:09:20.620
run once, and have a second
person approve the baseline.

141
00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:26.054
Then make three changes by hand: a secret
on an app that already has a certificate,

142
00:09:26.054 --> 00:09:29.595
a first secret on an app with
none, and a consent grant.

143
00:09:29.595 --> 00:09:33.949
Run the drift run, and check what
the fixture assumed against reality,

144
00:09:33.949 --> 00:09:35.858
like the exact activity names.

145
00:09:35.858 --> 00:09:39.219
Cross-check against Learn's
own audit log procedure,

146
00:09:39.219 --> 00:09:42.080
package and verify, then back it all out.

147
00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:49.518
Evidence fails in quiet ways,
so each of these has a guard.

148
00:09:49.518 --> 00:09:53.504
A baseline nobody approved lets
a malicious change become normal.

149
00:09:53.504 --> 00:09:58.518
A failed read can look like a fix, because
yesterday's findings simply disappear.

150
00:09:58.518 --> 00:10:02.368
A day nobody read leaves a gap
where a credential could be added.

151
00:10:02.368 --> 00:10:06.180
Trusting the Sentinel rule
misses the riskiest credentials,

152
00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:10.292
while alerting on every rotation
teaches people to ignore alerts.

153
00:10:10.292 --> 00:10:15.055
And a package edited or swapped after the
fact describes a run that never happened.

154
00:10:15.055 --> 00:10:20.230
The hash chain, the approved baseline
and honest gap reporting close each one.

155
00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.330
Here's what done looks like.

156
00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:28.780
Every run reads all
three parts and says so.

157
00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:31.767
The reader holds exactly
four read permissions.

158
00:10:31.767 --> 00:10:36.371
The baseline was approved by someone
else, each window picks up where the last

159
00:10:36.371 --> 00:10:39.104
ended, and every credential
added outside the

160
00:10:39.104 --> 00:10:41.693
pipeline has an
explanation or an incident.

161
00:10:41.693 --> 00:10:44.991
The review records arrive,
every package verifies,

162
00:10:44.991 --> 00:10:48.604
the chain has no breaks, and
the evidence store is locked.

163
00:10:48.604 --> 00:10:51.317
And the period summary
is signed and reviewed.

164
00:10:51.317 --> 00:10:53.290
That's the end of this workbook:

165
00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.842
every chapter's checks now
feed one verifiable period.

166
00:10:56.842 --> 00:10:58.010
Thanks for watching.
